// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT OR Apache-2.0
//
// The custom challenge triad, issue #114 criterion 6.
//
// Cognito's custom auth challenge model -- DefineAuthChallenge, CreateAuthChallenge,
// VerifyAuthChallengeResponse -- as one component exporting three functions.
//
// A SECOND WORLD IN THE SAME PACKAGE, AT THE SAME VERSION, and that is not an oversight.
// `token-customize.wit` states that the package version IS the interface version, so adding to
// the package without a bump needs the same argument adding an IMPORT needed there: a guest
// compiled against `token-customize-hook@0.1.0` sees that world unchanged, byte for byte. It
// gains nothing and loses nothing, because a world names exactly what it imports and exports and
// this file adds neither to it. What WOULD force a bump is changing a record, a function
// signature or a world already published -- which is the decade-ABI commitment, and it is about
// what an existing guest was compiled against rather than about what the package now contains.
//
// ONE COMPONENT, NOT THREE. Cognito splits the triad across three lambdas because that is
// what its deployment unit is; here they are three exports of one component, because the
// three halves of a factor share its logic and an operator adding a factor should deploy
// ONE artifact. Splitting them would also make "which define goes with which verify" a
// question an operator has to answer correctly on every deploy.
//
// WHAT MAKES IT A CUSTOM FACTOR RATHER THAN A SCRIPTED FORM: the host never learns what the
// challenge IS. It carries opaque parameters, renders the fields the guest names, and asks
// the guest whether the answer was right. Adding a second factor is another component and a
// journey that references it -- no host change, which is what criterion 6 asks for.

/// One input the challenge asks the user for.
///
/// Deliberately NOT the flow engine's node model. That model carries rendering concerns this
/// interface has no business fixing (groups, ordering ranks, autocomplete hints), and pinning
/// it here would make every change to how IronAuth renders a form a breaking change to every
/// deployed challenge component. A guest names WHAT it needs; the host decides how to draw it.
interface challenge-types {
  record field {
    /// The submission key this input's answer arrives under.
    name: string,
    /// The label to render. Localization is the host's, from the label as a key.
    label: string,
    /// Whether the value must be masked on screen and kept out of logs.
    ///
    /// A HINT THE HOST HONOURS, not a promise the guest enforces: the guest never sees the
    /// rendered form. It is here because the guest is the only party that knows whether the
    /// thing it is asking for is a secret.
    secret: bool,
  }

  /// One answer coming back from the user.
  record answer {
    /// The field name this answers.
    name: string,
    /// What the user typed.
    value: string,
  }

  /// What every call in the triad is told about the flow it is running in.
  ///
  /// NO SESSION, NO TOKENS, NO CREDENTIALS. A challenge component decides whether a user can
  /// prove something; it is not given the means to act as them. The subject is the pseudonymous
  /// id the flow has already established, and it is an `option` because a factor may run before
  /// one exists.
  record context {
    /// The payload version, explicit in every invocation, moving on its own schedule exactly
    /// as `token.customize`'s does.
    payload-version: u32,
    /// The pseudonymous subject this factor is challenging, when the flow has one.
    subject: option<string>,
    /// The client the login is for, so one component can serve several with different rules.
    client-id: string,
    /// How many challenges of this factor the flow has ALREADY completed, starting at zero.
    ///
    /// This is what lets `define` end a factor: a component that wants three rounds returns
    /// `challenge` while this is below three and `succeed` after. It counts COMPLETED rounds,
    /// not attempts at one round, so a wrong answer that the host re-renders does not advance it.
    round: u32,
    /// Whether the previous round's answer was accepted, absent on the first round.
    ///
    /// A component that ends the factor on a wrong answer reads this; one that allows retries
    /// ignores it and counts rounds instead.
    previous-passed: option<bool>,
  }
}

/// The three functions a custom factor implements.
interface custom-challenge {
  use challenge-types.{field, answer, context};

  /// What `define` decides happens next.
  ///
  /// The three outcomes are the whole state machine, and the host owns no other: it asks, and
  /// it does what it is told. That is what keeps a new factor out of the flow engine.
  variant decision {
    /// Issue (another) challenge: the host calls `create` next.
    challenge,
    /// The factor is satisfied. The host advances the flow.
    succeed,
    /// The factor has failed for good, with a reason for the audit trail.
    ///
    /// The reason is NOT rendered to the user. A factor that fails for a reason the user should
    /// see says so through `create`'s prompt on a further round; this string is for the operator
    /// reading why a login was refused.
    fail(string),
  }

  /// One challenge to put in front of the user.
  record challenge-spec {
    /// What to tell the user, as a localization key or literal prompt.
    prompt: string,
    /// The inputs to render, in the order given.
    fields: list<field>,
    /// What `verify` will need in order to check the answer, JSON-encoded.
    ///
    /// NEVER RENDERED AND NEVER SENT. The host holds it for the life of the round and hands it
    /// back to `verify`. This is where a one-time code, a nonce or an expected answer lives --
    /// putting it in `public-params` would publish the answer with the question.
    private-params: string,
    /// What the client may see alongside the fields, JSON-encoded.
    ///
    /// Rendered, so it is the half a component must assume an attacker reads.
    public-params: string,
  }

  /// Decide what happens next for this factor.
  define: func(ctx: context) -> result<decision, string>;

  /// Build the challenge to render.
  create: func(ctx: context) -> result<challenge-spec, string>;

  /// Check an answer against what `create` put aside.
  ///
  /// Returns whether the answer was RIGHT. The error case is the component failing to decide at
  /// all -- malformed parameters, an upstream it needed and could not reach -- which is a
  /// different fact from a wrong answer and gets the per-hook failure policy rather than another
  /// round.
  verify: func(
    ctx: context,
    private-params: string,
    answers: list<answer>,
  ) -> result<bool, string>;
}

/// The world a custom challenge component implements.
///
/// Imports the SAME capabilities `token-customize-hook` does, and for the same reasons: a
/// factor that checks a code against an upstream needs `fetch`, and one that signs or compares
/// against a shared key needs `secrets`. Both stay deny-by-default and per-hook.
world custom-challenge-hook {
  import secrets;
  import fetch;
  export custom-challenge;
}
